"Children do not grow at the same speed every year. From about age 2 to age 10, most children grow at a steady pace. Then come a few years of faster growth, which begin at a different age for each child, sometime between about 9 and 15. Later, the growth plates slowly close: bone replaces the last of the cartilage, and only a thin line is left. This usually happens in the middle or late teens. After that, you do not grow taller. Your bones are still alive, though, and they keep rebuilding themselves all your life.": "1596a178ddd5",
"Doctors can see growth plates on an X-ray. Bone shows up white, but cartilage hardly shows at all, so an open growth plate looks like a dark gap across the bone. Growth plates are the last part of a bone to harden, and they are weaker than the bone around them. That is why a child who falls hard and has a sore, swollen joint should see a doctor.": "0040b3e6b435",
"Inside a growth plate, cartilage cells divide again and again, and the new cells stack up in columns like coins. The oldest cells, on the side toward the middle of the bone, die, and hard bone takes their place. So the plate stays about the same thickness while the bone behind it grows longer, and the two ends of the bone move farther apart. A chemical messenger called growth hormone tells the cartilage cells to keep dividing. Your body releases growth hormone while you are in deep sleep.": "4559f672d236",
